/// z-scores.
///
/// Each value less the mean, divided by the standard deviation, so that the result has mean zero
/// and standard deviation one: `(x - mean(x)) / sd(x)`, with the sample standard deviation
/// (divisor n − 1) that `sd` gives. Both are taken over the values present, each with the
/// reducers' warning for the rows with `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of the Language Reference), and
/// those rows stay `NA`. A column with no spread, one value or all values equal, has no z-scores:
/// every row is `NA` with the warning `0 / 0` gives (W0205), never an error, so a grouped summary
/// over small groups still prints.
///
/// x: The column to standardise.
/// returns: A `Column<Float>` with mean zero and standard deviation one.
/// details: The sample standard deviation with divisor n − 1, as R's `scale` and `sd` use; standardising by the population deviation is `center(x) / sqrt(mean(center(x)^2))`, not offered.
/// reference: R Core Team, the `scale` function of the base package.
/// reference: Stata, `egen` with the `std()` function.
/// missing values: The mean and the standard deviation are taken over the values present, with a warning for the rows skipped, and a row that is `NA` stays `NA`.
/// coming from: d.add(z = standardize(wage)) | d$z <- as.numeric(scale(d$wage)) | egen z = std(wage)
/// coming from: R's `scale` gives a matrix of one column, with the mean and the deviation as attributes, which `as.numeric` takes away; both skip missing values, as here. Under `by:` the mean and the deviation are those of each group, as with `bysort year: egen z = std(wage)` in Stata.
/// related: To subtract the mean alone, use `center`.
/// related: For the standard deviation itself, use `sd`.
/// related: To cap extreme values before standardising, use `winsorize`.
/// see also: center, mean, sd
/// example: The wages of one year as z-scores.
/// > let w = dataset(wages).where(year == 2019).select([worker, year, wage]).add(z = standardize(wage))
/// > w.head(3)
/// warning[W0201]: 26 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 1)
/// frame · 3 rows × 4 variables · panel worker × year
///
///       worker   year    wage           z
///   1        1   2019   26.39   -0.286874
///   2        7   2019   23.39   -0.493649
///   3       13   2019   22.98   -0.521908
/// example: The result has standard deviation one.
/// > w.sd(z)
/// 1
/// warning[W0201]: 26 rows with NA in z were skipped (line 3)
/// example: Within each group with `by:`: each wage against the wages of its own year.
/// > dataset(wages).add(z = standardize(wage), by: year).where(worker == 1).select([worker, year, wage, z]).head(3)
/// frame · 3 rows × 4 variables · panel worker × year
///
///       worker   year    wage           z
///   1        1   2015   18.08   -0.659662
///   2        1   2016   17.28   -0.740958
///   3        1   2017   20.93   -0.523249
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 4)
/// example: A column with no spread has no z-scores: every row is `NA`, with a warning.
/// > frame(x = [5, 5, 5]).add(z = standardize(x))
/// frame · 3 rows × 2 variables
///
///       x    z
///   1   5   NA
///   2   5   NA
///   3   5   NA
/// warning[W0205]: 0 / 0 gave NA in 3 rows (line 5)
fn standardize(x: Column<Float>) -> Column<Float> {
    let f = frame(value = x)
    (x - f.mean(value)) / f.sd(value)
}
